Setting. Work over a base scheme S. Fix rho = [[0,1],[v,w]] and sigma = [[0,-1],[-v,1-w]], with v invertible. Then rho + sigma = diag(0,1) =: e, so c = Tr(rho+sigma) = 1. The next page writes sigma = [[0,-1],[v',w']] with v' = -v, w' = 1-w.

The previous page fixes the normalisations: rho sigma^{-1} = -eps_1 and sigma^{-1} rho = -eps_0. The one-parameter unipotent group eps_1(t) works out to the lower unipotent [[1,0],[t,1]]. This can be checked against the next page: from (46), eta^{-1} xi = [[1,0],[mu-1,1]].

The proposition of 'page 436 / prop 442'' (Grothendieck's original pagination) studies the commutator map u |-> ([u,rho],[u,sigma],det u), where [u,rho] = u rho u^{-1} rho^{-1}. Its source is the subgroup scheme B'_0 = {[[mu,lambda],[0,1]] : mu in G_m, lambda in G_a}, the affine 'ax+b' group (a Borel-type subgroup). Its target is the scheme X_{rho,sigma} of triples (xi, eta, mu) satisfying (41):
- det xi = det eta = 1;
- xi = eta eps_1(mu-1);
- Tr(xi rho) = Tr rho;
- Tr(eta sigma) = Tr sigma.

The last two conditions are tautological for commutators, since xi rho = u rho u^{-1} is a conjugate of rho.

This page eliminates eta and mu. Projecting (xi, eta, mu) |-> xi identifies X_{rho,sigma} with the scheme of xi = [[A,B],[C,D]] satisfying (42):
- AD - BC = 1;
- Tr(xi rho) = vB + C + wD = w (correctly computed);
- D a unit.

The inverse is mu = 1/D, eta = xi eps_1(1-mu). This scheme is visibly G_a x G_m: B and D are free with D invertible, C = w(1-D) - vB, and A = (1+BC)/D. That has the right dimension 2, matching B'_0.

The page then announces an explicit computation of the isomorphism (45) in the coordinates mu, lambda of B'_0, using the parameters v, w. This is carried out on the next page, (46)-(48), giving B = lambda/mu and D = 1/mu, i.e. u = [[1/D, B/D],[0,1]].

Purpose. This is part of the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'. Grothendieck is making explicit, in rank-2 matrix terms, the parametrisation of solutions of a commutator ('loop') equation for a pair of matrices adapted to the three-point configuration (rho, sigma, with rho sigma^{-1} unipotent up to sign). The parametrisation is by a two-dimensional affine group.
